---
import Sub from '../layouts/Sub.astro';
import { servicePageSchema } from '../data/site.js';

const description =
  'Concrete expansion joint replacement in St. George and Washington County. Failed joint filler lets water into the sub-base — we cut it out, clean the channel and re-fill it properly.';

const schema = servicePageSchema({
  path: '/expansion-joint-replacement',
  name: 'Expansion joint replacement',
  description,
  crumb: 'Expansion joints',
});
---

<Sub
  title="Expansion Joint Replacement | St. George UT | Armour Crete"
  description={description}
  crumb="Expansion joints"
  schema={schema}
>
  <Fragment slot="h1">The joint is doing<br />its job. The filler isn't.</Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="lede">
    Joints exist so the slab can move without cracking where it pleases. When the
    filler in them dries out, shrinks and splits, every joint becomes a drain —
    straight down into the sub-base your driveway is sitting on. We cut the old
    material out, clean the channel, and re-fill it so the slab can keep moving
    without letting anything in.
  </Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="aside">
    <dl class="facts">
      <div><dt>1/3"</dt><dd>Roughly how much a 50-foot slab moves across our seasons</dd></div>
      <div><dt>3</dt><dd>Steps — cut out, clean, re-fill. No caulk over the top of failure</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Free</dt><dd>On-site assessment, no obligation</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </Fragment>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div class="rv">
        <p class="eyebrow">Why it matters</p>
        <h2 class="display sec-h">Water under the slab<br />is the expensive kind.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose rv">
        <p>
          Concrete expands and contracts with temperature, and southern Utah gives
          it a hundred-degree annual swing to work with. The joints are where that
          movement is supposed to happen. But movement is exactly what kills the
          filler: it stretches and compresses season after season until it pulls
          away from the joint walls or splits down the middle.
        </p>
        <p>
          After that, every storm and every sprinkler cycle sends water through the
          open joint into the sub-base — the compacted material the slab depends
          on. Wash enough of it out and the slab settles, rocks and cracks, and at
          that point the repair is no longer a joint repair.
        </p>
        <p>
          Re-filling a failed joint costs a fraction of correcting a settled slab.
          It is also the first thing we check before
          <a class="tlink" href="/driveway-sealing">sealing a driveway</a> —
          sealing the surface while the joints are open is solving the small leak
          and ignoring the big one.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec sec-dark">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div class="rv">
        <p class="eyebrow eyebrow-lt">How we do it</p>
        <h2 class="display sec-h">Out, clean,<br />and back in.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="rv">
        <ul class="dotlist">
          <li>The failed filler is cut out completely — not topped up, not caulked over</li>
          <li>The channel is cleaned back to sound edges so the new material can bond</li>
          <li>The joint is re-filled with material that flexes with the slab's movement</li>
          <li>Done alongside sealing or on its own — whatever the surface actually needs</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="note" style="border-color: rgba(244,240,233,0.3); color: rgba(244,240,233,0.6);">
          Filler that is simply laid over the top of failed material fails again on
          the same schedule. Cutting it out is slower. It is also the job.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec-head rv">
        <p class="eyebrow">Expansion joint questions</p>
        <h2 class="display sec-h">Worth asking<br />before it settles.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="faq rv">
        <details class="qa">
          <summary>How do I know my joints have failed?</summary>
          <div class="qa-b"><p>
            Look along them. Filler that has sunk below the surface, pulled away
            from either edge, cracked down its length or gone hard and crumbly is
            no longer keeping water out. Weeds growing out of a joint are the
            surface's way of filing a report.
          </p></div>
        </details>
        <details class="qa">
          <summary>Can you just fill the gaps that look bad?</summary>
          <div class="qa-b"><p>
            We can, but we will tell you what we see first. Joint filler tends to
            age at the same rate across a slab, so one failed section usually means
            the rest are close behind — and doing them together costs less than
            doing them twice.
          </p></div>
        </details>
        <details class="qa">
          <summary>Does this get done before or after sealing?</summary>
          <div class="qa-b"><p>
            Before. Prep first, always — joints, cracks and surface correction all
            come ahead of any sealer. Nothing goes down over a problem.
          </p></div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Sub>
